        <div id="perform-tread" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Centr x HYROX Perform Tread in Detail</h2>
<p>
            The Perform Tread is the one Centr treadmill people search for by its full name, and it is the
            machine competitors run on at HYROX events. Centr describes it as a "motorless curved treadmill":
            there is no drive motor, you push the slatted belt with your feet, and speed follows where you run
            on the curve. Centr's store and Perform Better agree on its main figures: a 59 by 19-inch running
            area, eight magnetic resistance settings, a 396.8 lb (180 kg) maximum user and a 400 lb (181.6 kg)
            assembled weight.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What sets it apart</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Magnetic resistance.</strong> Many curved treadmills have no adjustable resistance at
            all. Eight levels let you make the belt heavier for strength work or lighter for fast running.</li>
            <li><strong>Sled-push station.</strong> The handlebar has several positions and converts to a
            push and pull station, so you can practise the HYROX sled stations' leg drive on the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Wider surface.</strong> At 19 inches, it is wider than the 17-inch surfaces of the
            TrueForm and <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Woodway</a> Curve LTG.</li>
            <li><strong>Long frame and belt warranty.</strong> For home use, a limited lifetime warranty on the
            frame and belt, 7 years on moving parts and 2 years on labour; for commercial use, 5 years on moving
            parts and 1 year on labour.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where the figures disagree</h3>
          <p>
            The height is listed as 67.3 inches on Centr's store and 59.2 inches (150.3 cm) on Perform
            Better's. Another retailer in search results gave 65 inches, and Garage Gym Reviews, also in
            search results, gave a length of 92.2 inches against the 74 inches on both Centr and Perform Better.
            We cannot tell which is right. Some sellers also call it the NMT.3, apparently a model code. Measure
            your space for the largest figures, and ask the seller for the shipping dimensions if your route in
            is tight.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price and where to buy</h3>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026 Centr and Perform Better both listed it at $6,999. A Costco listing in search
            results showed $5,999, which would be a real saving, but Costco refused our request and we could not
            confirm the current price or whether it was in stock. Centr's product data showed it as available,
            although its page also carried a "Back in stock soon" sign-up box. If you are a HYROX affiliate gym,
            Centr's page asks you to contact it, which suggests commercial pricing exists.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who should buy it</h3>
          <p>
            Buy the Perform Tread if you are training seriously for HYROX and want the machine you will race on,
            or if you run a gym that hosts HYROX training. For everyone else, it is hard to justify at twice the
            price of a TrueForm Trainer or AssaultRunner Pro. If your interest is sled-push work on a budget,
            the <a href="/tru-grit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Tru Grit Runner</a>'s two sled settings cost about a third as much. Independent coverage is thin:
            the main review we found, from Garage Gym Reviews, praised the curved design and adjustable
            resistance and criticised the price, weight and size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runr" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Centr Runr and Runr-S in Detail</h2>
<p>
            The Runr and Runr-S are folding motorized treadmills sharing one frame. Centr lists both with a
            3.0 HP brushless DC motor, 1 to 10 mph, 1 to 12% incline in 1% steps, a 20 by 55-inch running
            surface, a 300 lb maximum user, a 6-inch step-up, a self-lubricating belt, and 70 by 35.7 by
            47.5 inches and 194 lb assembled. The Runr has an LED console with quick keys and a 5-colour <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>heart
            rate zone</a> display; the Runr-S adds a 10-inch touchscreen with Netflix, YouTube and Disney+ apps,
            Bluetooth speakers and smartphone mirroring, plus 25 programs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">"Folding, tool-less assembly"</h3>
          <p>
            The phrase comes from Costco's listing name, "Centr Runr Folding Tool-less Assembly Treadmill".
            Centr says both models need no tools to set up, and that they fold. Garage Gym Reviews, in search
            results, cautioned that the Runr-S is too wide to pass through most doorways unless it is folded and
            turned on its side, a two-person job. Tool-free does not mean light: at 194 lb it needs two people
            to move.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The incline conflict</h3>
          <p>
            Centr's specification table lists incline as 1 to 12%. Costco's listing, as shown in search
            results, gives a 10% maximum, and Garage Gym Reviews gives 0 to 12% for the Runr-S. The difference
            may be a listing error or a different production run; check the box or the console before relying
            on 12% for incline walking. We have left incline out of the comparison table.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How they compare for the money</h3>
          <p>
            A 55-inch deck, a 10 mph top speed and a 300 lb rating are modest at $1,299 to $1,699. Runners over
            about 6 feet, or anyone doing fast intervals, will find 55 inches short; our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for tall runners</a> explains why deck length matters at speed. What the Runr has that many rivals do not is a lifetime
            warranty on both the frame and the motor. For a walking and jogging treadmill that folds away, that
            is a fair reason to choose it; for running, compare our <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a> first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Centr's "Natural Stride Technology", which it says senses your speed and adjusts the belt to match,
            is not something we can evaluate without testing. Treat it as a feature to try in a store if you
            can, not a reason to buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspire-series" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Inspire Series Treadmills on Centr’s Store</h2>
<p>
            Centr's US store also sells four Inspire Series treadmills, made by its sister company. We include
            them because a buyer looking at the Runr will see them on the same site. The figures here come from
            Centr's listings alone, which we have not cross-checked, so treat them as a starting point.
          </p>
